# Topological Data Analysis (TDA) using Eirene.jl

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:julia_elixir, path: Path.expand("..", __DIR__)},
    {:kino, "~> 0.19.0"},
    {:vega_lite, "~> 0.1.11"},
    {:kino_vega_lite, "~> 0.1.13"}
  ],
  config: [
    julia_elixir: [start_default_session: false]
  ]
)
```

## About This Notebook

This notebook uses `julia_elixir` to drive
[`Eirene.jl`](https://github.com/henselman-petrusek/Eirene.jl), a Julia package
for persistent homology. It follows the spirit of Eirene's
[`EXAMPLES.md`](https://github.com/henselman-petrusek/Eirene.jl/blob/master/EXAMPLES.md):

* load the bundled noisy-circle point cloud
* compute persistent homology
* inspect barcode and persistence summaries
* try generated point clouds and distance matrices
* visualize ETF-friendly summaries with VegaLite

The original examples use PlotlyJS plotting helpers. This notebook uses
VegaLite on the Elixir side instead, which fits the current JuliaElixir
prototype well: Julia does the topology and returns compact rows for plotting.

## Install Julia Dependencies

Eirene's registered release may not resolve on newer Julia versions because of
old dependency bounds. This notebook installs Eirene from the current GitHub
repository, whose `Project.toml` has newer compatibility bounds.

First run can take several minutes because Eirene depends on PlotlyJS/WebIO and
other packages.

```elixir
IO.puts("""
First-run notice
Internet access and a writable cache are required. Setup downloads Julia
(about 220–320 MB) and Eirene's dependencies; allow several GB of free disk space.
Installation and precompilation can take many minutes. Let setup finish;
later runs reuse cached downloads and precompiled packages.
Notebook runs are verified on Linux x86-64; native macOS, Windows and ARM64
notebook validation is still pending.
""")

# Keep this notebook's Julia dependencies separate from other projects.
cache = to_string(:filename.basedir(:user_cache, ~c"julia_elixir"))
System.put_env("JULIA_PROJECT", Path.join(cache, "notebooks/eirene"))
:ok = JuliaElixir.setup(packages: ["Eirene"])
```

## Start A Julia Session

```elixir
defmodule EireneNotebook do
  def ensure_started(name) do
    case Process.whereis(name) do
      nil -> JuliaElixir.start(name: name, startup_timeout: 20_000)
      pid -> {:ok, pid}
    end
  end

  def stop(name) do
    case Process.whereis(name) do
      nil -> :ok
      _pid -> JuliaElixir.stop(name)
    end
  end
end

{:ok, pid} = EireneNotebook.ensure_started(:eirene_julia)
pid
```

## Load Eirene

```elixir
JuliaElixir.eval!(
  """
  using Eirene
  using DelimitedFiles
  using Statistics

  "Eirene loaded"
  """,
  server: :eirene_julia,
  timeout: 120_000
)
```

```elixir
JuliaElixir.eval!("Eirene.example_function()", server: :eirene_julia)
```

## Noisy Circle Dataset

Eirene ships a noisy circle CSV in `src/examples/noisycircle.csv`. The original
example locates it with `eirenefilepath("noisycircle")` and loads it with
`DelimitedFiles.readdlm`.

```elixir
JuliaElixir.eval!(
  """
  circle_path = eirenefilepath("noisycircle")
  circle_points = readdlm(circle_path, ',')

  circle_rows = [
    Dict(:x => circle_points[1, i], :y => circle_points[2, i], :point => i)
    for i in 1:size(circle_points, 2)
  ]

  Dict(
    :path => circle_path,
    :rows => size(circle_points, 1),
    :cols => size(circle_points, 2),
    :first_point => circle_points[:, 1],
    :x_range => [minimum(circle_points[1, :]), maximum(circle_points[1, :])],
    :y_range => [minimum(circle_points[2, :]), maximum(circle_points[2, :])]
  )
  """,
  server: :eirene_julia
)
```

```elixir
circle_rows =
  JuliaElixir.eval!(
    """
    circle_rows
    """,
    server: :eirene_julia
  )

VegaLite.new(width: 420, height: 420, title: "Noisy circle point cloud")
|> VegaLite.data_from_values(circle_rows)
|> VegaLite.mark(:point, filled: true, opacity: 0.8, size: 36)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "x", type: :quantitative, scale: [zero: false])
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "y", type: :quantitative, scale: [zero: false])
```

## Compute Persistent Homology

This cell mirrors the original example:

```julia
C = eirene(pointcloud, model = "pc")
```

The result is a large Julia dictionary, so we keep it in the Julia session as
`circle_C` and return only a compact summary.

```elixir
JuliaElixir.eval!(
  """
  circle_C = eirene(circle_points, model="pc")

  Dict(
    :result_type => string(typeof(circle_C)),
    :key_count => length(keys(circle_C)),
    :all_keys => collect(keys(circle_C)),
    :has_cyclerep => haskey(circle_C, "cyclerep"),
    :input_metadata => Dict(
      :maxrad => circle_C["input"]["maxrad"],
      :minrad => circle_C["input"]["minrad"],
      :numrad => circle_C["input"]["numrad"]
    )
  )
  """,
  server: :eirene_julia,
  timeout: 180_000
)
```

We can inspect individual entries of the dictionary in the Julia session and
decode them on demand. `circle_C["input"]` contains Julia `Inf` and `-Inf`
values. JuliaElixir maps those to Elixir atoms because BEAM ETF decoding does
not accept infinity/NaN float payloads.

```elixir
JuliaElixir.eval!(
  """
  circle_C["input"]
  """,
  server: :eirene_julia
)
|> Map.take(["maxrad", "minrad", "numrad", "fastop", "model", "record"])
```

Alternatively, we can decode the whole dictionary and inspect it in Elixir (maybe not the most optimal for large ones):

```elixir
circle_C = JuliaElixir.eval!("circle_C", server: :eirene_julia)
circle_C["input"] |> Map.take(["maxrad", "minrad", "numrad", "fastop", "model", "record"])
```

## Barcode Summaries

Eirene exposes barcode data through `barcode(C, dim=...)`. Dimension 0 captures
connected components. Dimension 1 captures loops; the noisy circle should have
one prominent long-lived 1-dimensional class.

```elixir
JuliaElixir.eval!(
  """
  function barcode_summary(C, dim; n=5)
      B = barcode(C, dim=dim)
      lifetimes = B[:, 2] .- B[:, 1]
      order = sortperm(lifetimes, rev=true)
      top = order[1:min(n, length(order))]

      Dict(
        :dim => dim,
        :rows => size(B, 1),
        :cols => size(B, 2),
        :top_class_indices => top,
        :top_births => B[top, 1],
        :top_deaths => B[top, 2],
        :top_lifetimes => lifetimes[top]
      )
  end

  function barcode_rows(C, dim)
      B = barcode(C, dim=dim)
      [
        Dict(
          :class => i,
          :dim => dim,
          :birth => B[i, 1],
          :death => B[i, 2],
          :lifetime => B[i, 2] - B[i, 1]
        )
        for i in 1:size(B, 1)
      ]
  end

  Dict(
    :h0 => barcode_summary(circle_C, 0),
    :h1 => barcode_summary(circle_C, 1)
  )
  """,
  server: :eirene_julia
)
```

```elixir
h1_barcode_rows =
  JuliaElixir.eval!("barcode_rows(circle_C, 1)", server: :eirene_julia)
  |> Enum.sort_by(& &1.lifetime, :desc)

VegaLite.new(width: 620, height: 280, title: "Noisy circle H1 lifetimes")
|> VegaLite.data_from_values(h1_barcode_rows)
|> VegaLite.mark(:bar)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "class", type: :ordinal, sort: "-y", title: "class")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "lifetime", type: :quantitative, title: "death - birth")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "lifetime", type: :quantitative, legend: nil)
```

```elixir
top_h1_rows = Enum.take(h1_barcode_rows, 12)

VegaLite.new(width: 620, height: 300, title: "Top H1 barcode intervals")
|> VegaLite.data_from_values(top_h1_rows)
|> VegaLite.mark(:bar)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "class", type: :ordinal, sort: "-x", title: "class")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "birth", type: :quantitative, title: "birth")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x2, "death")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "lifetime", type: :quantitative)
```

## Interpolate Elixir Options Into Julia

`JuliaElixir.literal/1` is useful when a Livebook widget or Elixir variable
controls the Julia computation.

```elixir
opts = %{
  model: "pc",
  maxdim: 1
}

JuliaElixir.eval!(
  """
  opts = #{JuliaElixir.literal(opts)}

  circle_C_from_elixir_opts = eirene(
    circle_points,
    model=opts[:model],
    maxdim=opts[:maxdim]
  )

  barcode_summary(circle_C_from_elixir_opts, 1)
  """,
  server: :eirene_julia,
  timeout: 180_000
)
```

## Generated Clouds

Eirene also includes generators such as `noisycircle()`, `noisycircle3()`,
`torus(...)`, `noisytorus(...)`, `sphere()`, and several distance-matrix
examples.

```elixir
JuliaElixir.eval!(
  """
  generated_circle = noisycircle()
  generated_circle3 = noisycircle3()
  small_torus = torus(m=12, n=8, mrad=1, nrad=0.2)

  small_torus_rows = [
    Dict(:x => small_torus[1, i], :y => small_torus[2, i], :z => small_torus[3, i], :point => i)
    for i in 1:size(small_torus, 2)
  ]

  Dict(
    :noisycircle_size => collect(size(generated_circle)),
    :noisycircle3_size => collect(size(generated_circle3)),
    :small_torus_size => collect(size(small_torus)),
    :small_torus_first_point => small_torus[:, 1]
  )
  """,
  server: :eirene_julia
)
```

```elixir
small_torus_rows =
  JuliaElixir.eval!("small_torus_rows", server: :eirene_julia)

VegaLite.new(width: 420, height: 360, title: "Generated torus projected to x/y")
|> VegaLite.data_from_values(small_torus_rows)
|> VegaLite.mark(:point, filled: true, size: 60)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "x", type: :quantitative, scale: [zero: false])
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "y", type: :quantitative, scale: [zero: false])
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "z", type: :quantitative, title: "z")
```

## Distance Matrix Examples

Eirene's examples also mention distance matrices such as matching complexes,
chessboard complexes, and random zero-dimensional examples.

_Warning:_ The upstream `matchingcomplex_symmat(m, n)` helper currently errors on newer
Julia/Eirene dependency combinations because it references an unqualified
`combinations` function.

```elixir
JuliaElixir.eval!(
  """
  # mc = matchingcomplex_symmat(m, n) # errors in v1.4.0
  chessboard = chessboardcomplex_symmat(numrows=3, numcols=5)
  zero_d = zerodrandmat(10)

  Dict(
    :chessboard_rows => size(chessboard, 1),
    :chessboard_cols => size(chessboard, 2),
    :zerod_rows => size(zero_d, 1),
    :zerod_cols => size(zero_d, 2),
    :zerod_first_row => zero_d[1, :]
  )
  """,
  server: :eirene_julia
)
```

```elixir
zero_d_rows =
  JuliaElixir.eval!(
    """
    [
      Dict(:i => i, :j => j, :distance => zero_d[i, j])
      for i in 1:size(zero_d, 1)
      for j in 1:size(zero_d, 2)
    ]
    """,
    server: :eirene_julia
  )

VegaLite.new(width: 360, height: 360, title: "Random zero-dimensional distance matrix")
|> VegaLite.data_from_values(zero_d_rows)
|> VegaLite.mark(:rect)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "i", type: :ordinal, title: "row")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "j", type: :ordinal, title: "column")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "distance", type: :quantitative)
```

## Optional PlotlyJS Comparison

Eirene's native examples still expose PlotlyJS helpers like `ezplot_pjs`,
`plotbarcode_pjs`, `plotpersistencediagram_pjs`, and `plotclassrep_pjs`. This
prototype notebook does not need them, but this cell shows they can still run
inside Julia state if your environment supports them.

```elixir
JuliaElixir.eval!(
  """
  circle_barcode_plot = plotbarcode_pjs(circle_C, dim=1)

  Dict(
    :plot_type => string(typeof(circle_barcode_plot)),
    :note => "Plot object is stored in Julia as circle_barcode_plot"
  )
  """,
  server: :eirene_julia,
  timeout: 120_000
)
```

Otherwise, we can always plot barcodes (among others) with Kino:

```elixir
circle_h1_barcode_for_kino =
  JuliaElixir.eval!(
    """
    barcode_rows(circle_C, 1)
    """,
    server: :eirene_julia
  )
  |> Enum.sort_by(& &1.lifetime, :desc)
  |> Enum.with_index(1)
  |> Enum.map(fn {row, rank} -> Map.put(row, :rank, rank) end)

VegaLite.new(width: 700, height: 360, title: "Kino/VegaLite H1 barcode for circle_C")
|> VegaLite.data_from_values(circle_h1_barcode_for_kino)
|> VegaLite.mark(:bar, height: 4)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:y, "rank",
  type: :ordinal,
  sort: "ascending",
  title: "class ranked by lifetime"
)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x, "birth", type: :quantitative, title: "filtration value")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:x2, "death")
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:color, "lifetime",
  type: :quantitative,
  title: "lifetime"
)
|> VegaLite.encode_field(:tooltip, "class", type: :ordinal)
```

## Stop Session

```elixir
EireneNotebook.stop(:eirene_julia)
```
